WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.060
Speaker 1: I'm going to tell you today how&nbsp;
the rich and powerful really control American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:12.660
politics. And it's so funny because it sounds like&nbsp;
a conspiracy headline. The truth about the elites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:18.720
controlling our political system. But that's the&nbsp;
joke. That's what's funny about it. For all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:25.560
the people looking for the conspiracies for how&nbsp;
American politics is controlled, oh, it's a cabal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:32.400
of pedophiles who trade kids and drink the blood&nbsp;
of children in exchange for favors, maybe outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.820
a pizza shop, I don't know. Or it's an elite&nbsp;
of Jewish men, maybe with space lasers or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:45.960
voting machine people. It's out in the open. It's&nbsp;
a conspiracy out in the open. The real way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.960
the rich and powerful control American politics&nbsp;
is publicly known. And we're going to go over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:59.760
it here. You don't need to look for the hidden&nbsp;
conspiracy. Let's start with campaign financing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:06.480
The cost of running for office national office&nbsp;
in the US is huge and it continues going up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.660
which means candidates rely on donations from&nbsp;
corporations and wealthy people in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:18.600
fund their campaigns. On average, the candidate&nbsp;
that raises more money wins and the donors expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:25.440
something in return for their investment. It's&nbsp;
out in the open. It's legalized bribery when a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:31.140
candidate takes money from a donor. The donor on&nbsp;
average has influence over the candidate. Doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.880
mean they're meeting in person and saying,&nbsp;
here's what I need you to do. But it exerts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:41.280
some influence for sure. And they can expect,&nbsp;
at least for the candidate to listen to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.440
to take their concerns into account when making&nbsp;
policy decisions. And we find it in the facts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:52.920
We find there's that famous Princeton study from a&nbsp;
few years ago which found the legislative desires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:59.820
of the wealthy and of corporations are much more&nbsp;
likely to be pursued as legislation and made into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:06.300
law by our elected officials than the concerns&nbsp;
of just the average person. So it works. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:12.000
out in the open. You don't need to search for the&nbsp;
secret way in which influence is being wielded.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:19.620
It's not just politicians influenced by money. The&nbsp;
policymaking process itself is heavily influenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:25.800
by special interests and lobbyists, which then can&nbsp;
come in and say, Focus on this rather than that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:32.340
These are people who are paid. It's all legal to&nbsp;
advocate for certain policies on behalf of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:38.700
clients. They often have access to lawmakers that&nbsp;
you and I don't. They can make their voices heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:44.820
in ways that average people can't. And so it's&nbsp;
another part of the feedback loop that leads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:50.400
policies that may not be in the public interest,&nbsp;
but they benefit the select few that are using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.580
their money to get that influence. But that's&nbsp;
money. There's more than just money that gives the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:03.300
rich and powerful control over American politics.&nbsp;
They also have access to the best strategists who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:10.680
can help them shape public opinion. Focus group&nbsp;
polling. Market research. And they can use those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:16.980
strategists to influence the outcome of elections.&nbsp;
You and I don't have that. We can phone bank, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:23.520
can donate paltry sums, but we don't have access&nbsp;
to that. The political strategists are experts at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.280
crafting messages, framing issues in ways that&nbsp;
are favorable to their clients and to theirs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.260
their desires. They've got the data, the market&nbsp;
research to understand what will work on voters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:40.320
and that's another reason they are more likely&nbsp;
to succeed. And then the third prong of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:48.060
is media and access to the media. Media can be&nbsp;
used in and of itself to shape public opinion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:54.180
to influence the political discourse, to include&nbsp;
certain topics and exclude others. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.180 --> 00:04:01.560
the money is used in order to influence media. And&nbsp;
media plays a major role in American politics. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:08.640
shouldn't. We should accurately assess what the&nbsp;
media is and isn't. But we have no choice if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.220
we're connected to reality, to acknowledge that&nbsp;
media generates or wields the power to decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.420
what stories get covered in which stories don't,&nbsp;
how are they presented? What's the framing? Can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:24.240
something be inserted or excluded from the public&nbsp;
discourse, which has a dramatic impact on both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:30.360
elections and also on legislation passing or not?&nbsp;
When it comes to the wealthy and the powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:36.540
they often get favorable coverage either through&nbsp;
their use of strategists and PR people, through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.760
their use of advertising dollars. And this can&nbsp;
make it really a difficult thing, an uphill battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:47.640
for just the average person to have their voice&nbsp;
heard in the same way. So it's all in the open.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:56.280
Whenever you hear a conspiracy theory about the&nbsp;
secret campaign to wield influence and the elites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:03.180
control, you could say the white. It's it's all&nbsp;
happening in the open. There's no need to find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:09.900
secret way in which it's being done now to counter&nbsp;
that influence. What can be done? Everybody has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:16.080
be voting as a starting point. Don't throw away&nbsp;
the one vote you have. We should be educated and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.760
aware of the ways in which this is taking place.&nbsp;
Please, if you don't even know the mechanisms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:26.940
it's going to be hard to fight against them and&nbsp;
then support candidates and policies that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:32.880
number one in the public interest. But number&nbsp;
two would work to maybe defuze or reduce this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:39.480
disproportionate power that the rich, wealthy&nbsp;
and corporations have. That's the way it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.180
You don't need to find some secret conspiracy&nbsp;
because it's all happening out in the open

